\ ff-test: lines budget=1000000
\ Control structures are tracked on a stack of their own, and every
\ closer is checked against what it closes.
\
\ They used to be bare heap offsets on the data stack: `then` patched
\ whatever number it found there (`[ 100000 ] then` wrote far outside the
\ word), `begin then` patched a cell that was never a branch, and an `if`
\ still open at `;` left its branch pointing past the end of the word.

: p 32 emit . ;

\ A closer that doesn't match, and a structure left open, are errors.
: t1 [ 100000 ] then ;
: t2 begin then ;
: t3 if ;
: t4 do until ;
: t5 1 if loop ;
: t6 begin repeat ;
clear

\ A structure can't straddle a `{ }` scope: its branch would skip the
\ scope's entry or exit.
: t7 1 if { ( -- ) then } ;
: t8 { ( -- ) 1 if } ;
: t9 begin { ( -- ) again } ;

\ The data stack is the program's while compiling: `literal` works inside
\ a structure.
: t10 1 if [ 2 3 + ] literal else 0 then ;
t10 p cr

\ Any number of `while`s, the ones after the first closed by `then`.
: classify begin dup 10 < while dup 5 <> while 1 + repeat ." "five" drop else ." "ten" drop then ;
3 classify cr
7 classify cr
see classify

\ `leave` needs a loop.
: t11 leave ;

\ `exit` from inside a loop drops the loop's parameters first; it used
\ to return through them as if they were a return frame.
: first-over 0 do i i * 10 > if i exit then loop -1 ;
5 first-over p 3 first-over p cr
: caller 5 first-over 100 + ;
caller p cr
: find-pair 5 0 do 5 0 do i j * 6 = if i j + exit then loop loop -1 ;
find-pair p cr

\ Leaving a scope early closes it as `}` would, checking its outputs; the
\ barrier used to stay up in the caller.
clear
: clamp0 { ( n -- m ) n 0 < if 0 exit then n } ;
: use-clamp -5 clamp0 7 clamp0 + ;
use-clamp p depth p cr
: bad-clamp { ( n -- m ) n 0 < if exit then n } ;
-1 bad-clamp
clear
: upto3 0 swap 0 do { ( c -- d ) c 1 + dup 3 = if leave then } loop ;
10 upto3 p 2 upto3 p depth p cr

\ `see` shows those exits as written.
see first-over
see clamp0

\ `does>` ends the defining word, so it can't stand inside a loop, whose
\ parameters it would take for the return address.
: t12 create 3 0 do does> loop ;
